        "problem": "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/5e3f9d31bc4cb5687d784097679861644cc55ce667ea1469d0d790bfe879706f.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4(2-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
